E = {
146: ("Homeland: its kinds and when it ends", [
 "One's original homeland is cancelled only by another like it. A homeland of residence is cancelled by another like it, by travel, and by an original homeland.",
 "The original homeland is where he was born, or where he married, or where, without marrying, he intends to live and not move away from.",
 "A homeland of residence is a place where he intended to stay for half a month or more. The careful scholars did not count a 'homeland of dwelling', which is where he intends to stay less than half a month.",
]),
147: ("How the sick person prays", [
 "If standing is wholly impossible for a sick person, or difficult because of severe pain, or he fears his illness will increase or be slow to heal, he prays sitting with ruku' and prostration, sitting however he wishes according to the soundest view; otherwise he stands as much as he can.",
 "If ruku' and prostration are impossible, he prays sitting with gestures, making the gesture for prostration lower than that for ruku'; if he does not make it lower, it is not valid. Nothing is raised to his face for him to prostrate on; if this is done and he lowers his head, it is valid, otherwise not.",
 "If sitting is difficult, he gestures lying on his back or on his side, and the first is better. A pillow is put under his head so that his face is towards the qiblah, not the sky, and he should raise his knees if he can, so as not to stretch his legs towards the qiblah.",
 "If gesturing is impossible, the prayers are postponed for him as long as he understands what is said to him; in al-Hidayah it says this is the sound view. The author of al-Hidayah stated in al-Tajnis wa'l-Mazid that the make-up is dropped if his inability to gesture lasts more than five prayers, even if he understands what is said; Qadi Khan declared this sound, as did al-Muhit; Shaykh al-Islam and Fakhr al-Islam chose it; al-Zahiriyyah said it is the apparent narration and fatwa is on it; al-Khulasah said it is the chosen view; it was declared sound in al-Yanabi' and al-Bada'i'; and al-Walwaliji stated it firmly. May Allah have mercy on them.",
]),
148: ("Various rulings", ["He does not gesture with his eyes, heart or eyebrows. If he is able to stand but unable to bow and prostrate, he prays sitting with gestures. If illness comes on during the prayer, he completes it as he is able, even with gestures, according to the well-known view. If he was praying sitting with ruku' and prostration and recovers, he continues standing; but not if he was praying by gestures. Whoever is insane or unconscious for five prayers makes them up; if more, he does not."]),
149: ("When a bequest is not required", ["If the sick person dies without having been able to pray even by gestures, he does not have to make a bequest for those prayers, even if they are few. Likewise for fasts that a traveller or sick person missed, if they died before becoming resident or healthy."]),
150: ("When he must make a bequest", ["He must make a bequest for what he was able to do but remained a debt upon him."]),
151: ("How the obligation is discharged (isqat)", [
 "His heir gives on his behalf, from a third of what he left, for each day of fasting and for each prayer, including witr, half a sa' of wheat or its value.",
 "If he did not make a bequest and his heir volunteers it on his behalf, that is permitted. It is not valid for him to fast or pray on his behalf.",
]),
152: ("The method of freeing the deceased from his debt", ["If what he bequeathed does not cover what he owes, that amount is given to a poor person, and so much is dropped from the deceased. Then the poor person gives it as a gift to the heir, who takes possession of it, then gives it to the poor person again, and so much more is dropped; then the poor person gives it back to the heir, who takes it and gives it again, and so on until all the prayers and fasts the deceased owed are dropped."]),
153: ("To whom the fidyah is given", ["The fidyah for prayers may be given all at once to one person, unlike the expiation for an oath. Allah, glorified and exalted, knows best."]),
154: ("The ruling on keeping the order", ["Keeping the order between a missed prayer and the current prayer, and between missed prayers, is required."]),
155: ("What drops the order", [
 "It is dropped by one of three things:",
 "the recommended time becoming short, according to the soundest view; forgetting; and when the missed prayers reach six, not counting witr. Witr does not count towards dropping it, though its order is required.",
]),
156: ("Various rulings", [
 "The order does not return when the missed prayers become few again, nor because of a recent missed prayer after six old ones, according to the soundest view in both. So if he prays an obligatory prayer while remembering a missed prayer, even witr, his obligatory prayer is invalid in a suspended way. If the time of the fifth prayer he has prayed after the missed one, while remembering it, ends, all of them become valid, and they are not cancelled by making up the missed prayer afterwards. If he makes up the missed prayer before the time of the fifth ends, the obligatory character of what he prayed while remembering it is cancelled and it becomes voluntary.",
 "When the missed prayers are many, he must specify each prayer. If he wants to make it easy for himself, he intends the first Zuhr he owes, or the last; likewise with fasts from two Ramadans, according to one of two different verdicts of soundness.",
 "One who accepts Islam in enemy territory is excused for not knowing the laws.",
]),
157: ("When one praying breaks off the prayer and when not", [
 "If he begins an obligatory prayer alone and the congregation begins, he breaks off and joins it, if he has not yet prostrated in what he began, or if he has prostrated in a prayer that is not four rak'ahs.",
 "If he has prostrated in a four-rak'ah prayer, he adds a second rak'ah and gives salam, so that the two rak'ahs become voluntary for him; then he joins with the obligatory intention.",
 "If he has prayed three, he completes it, then joins them with a voluntary intention, except in 'Asr.",
 "If he has stood for the third and the iqamah is called before he prostrates, he breaks off standing with one salam, according to the soundest view.",
 "If he is in the sunnah of Jumu'ah and the khatib comes out, or in the sunnah of Zuhr and the iqamah is called, he gives salam after two rak'ahs, which is the stronger view, then makes up the sunnah after the obligatory prayer.",
 "Whoever comes while the imam is in the obligatory prayer joins him and does not busy himself with the sunnah instead, except in Fajr if he is sure he will not miss the prayer; if he is not sure, he leaves the sunnah.",
]),
158: ("Making up the sunnah", ["The sunnah of Fajr is made up only if it is missed together with the obligatory prayer. The sunnah before Zuhr is made up within its time, before its two rak'ahs (after the obligatory)."]),
159: ("The congregation and its merit", ["By catching one rak'ah he has not prayed Zuhr in congregation, but he has gained its merit. There is a difference of opinion about one who catches three."]),
160: ("Various rulings", [
 "He prays voluntary prayer before the obligatory prayer if he is sure he will not miss the time; otherwise not. Whoever reaches his imam in ruku', says the takbir and stands until the imam raises his head, has not caught the rak'ah. If he bows before his imam, after the imam has recited enough for the prayer to be valid, and his imam then joins him in it, it is valid; otherwise not.",
 "It is disliked to leave a masjid in which the adhan has been called until he prays, unless he leads another congregation. If he leaves after praying alone it is not disliked, unless the congregation begins before he leaves, in Zuhr and 'Isha', in which case he joins them with a voluntary intention. He does not pray a prayer like it after praying it.",
]),
161: ("Prostration of forgetfulness: its ruling and cause", ["Two prostrations with tashahhud and salam are wajib for leaving a wajib act by forgetfulness, even if it happens more than once."]),
162: ("Leaving a wajib act on purpose", [
 "If he left it on purpose, he is sinful and must repeat the prayer to make up its deficiency.",
 "The prostration of forgetfulness is not made for deliberate omission; it is said, except in three cases: leaving the first sitting, delaying a prostration of the first rak'ah to the end of the prayer, and thinking deliberately until it distracts him from a pillar.",
]),
163: ("When the prostration is made", ["It is sunnah to make the prostration of forgetfulness after the salam, with one salam to the right being enough according to the soundest view. If he prostrates before the salam, it is mildly disliked."]),
164: ("When it is dropped", ["The prostration of forgetfulness is dropped if the sun rises after the salam in Fajr, or turns red in 'Asr, or if something happens after the salam that prevents continuing the prayer."]),
165: ("Who must prostrate", [
 "The follower must prostrate because of his imam's forgetfulness, but not his own. The late-comer prostrates with his imam, then stands to make up what he missed; and if he forgets in what he is making up, he prostrates for that as well. Not so the lahiq (one who joined from the start but missed part).",
 "The imam does not make the prostration of forgetfulness in Jumu'ah and the two 'Ids.",
]),
166: ("Various rulings", [
 "One who forgets the first sitting of an obligatory prayer returns to it as long as he has not stood up fully, according to the apparent narration, which is the soundest. The follower is like one praying voluntarily: he returns even if he has stood up fully. If he returns when he is nearer to standing, he makes the prostration of forgetfulness; if nearer to sitting, he does not have to, according to the soundest view. If he returns after standing up fully, verdicts differ as to whether his prayer is invalid.",
 "If he forgets the final sitting, he returns to it as long as he has not prostrated (in an extra rak'ah), and prostrates for delaying the obligatory sitting. If he has prostrated, his obligatory prayer becomes voluntary, and he may add a sixth rak'ah if he wishes, even in 'Asr, and a fourth in Fajr; adding them is not disliked according to the sound view, and he does not prostrate for forgetfulness according to the soundest view. If he sat for the final sitting and then stood up, he returns and gives salam without repeating the tashahhud. If he prostrates (in the extra rak'ah), his obligatory prayer is not cancelled; he adds another so that the two extra become voluntary for him, and prostrates for forgetfulness. If he prostrated for forgetfulness in a pair of voluntary rak'ahs, he does not, as a recommendation, build another pair on it; if he does, he repeats the prostration of forgetfulness, according to the chosen view. If someone who owes a prostration of forgetfulness gives salam and another person then follows him, it is valid if he makes the prostration, and otherwise not. He makes the prostration even if he gave salam deliberately intending to end the prayer, as long as he has not turned from the qiblah or spoken. If one praying four or three rak'ahs thinks he has completed it and gives salam, then learns he prayed two, he completes it and prostrates for forgetfulness. If he thinks for a long time, without giving salam, until he becomes sure, then if it was the length of a pillar the prostration of forgetfulness is wajib; otherwise not.",
]),
167: ("When doubt invalidates the prayer", ["The prayer is invalid because of doubt about the number of its rak'ahs if it happens before completing it and it is the first time he has had such doubt, or doubt is not usual for him. Doubt after the salam is not considered, unless he becomes sure he left something out."]),
168: ("Frequent doubt", ["If he doubts often, he acts on his strongest opinion. If he has no strongest opinion, he takes the lesser number, and sits after every rak'ah he thinks could be the last of his prayer."]),
169: ("Prostration of recitation: its cause, ruling and time", ["Its cause is recitation, for the reciter and the listener, according to the sound view. It is wajib, with room to delay, if it is not in the prayer; delaying it is mildly disliked. It is wajib on whoever recites a verse, even in Persian. Reciting the word of prostration with a word before or after it from its verse is like reciting the verse, according to the sound view."]),
170: ("The verses of prostration", ["Its verses are fourteen: in al-A'raf, al-Ra'd, al-Nahl, al-Isra', Maryam, the first in al-Hajj, al-Furqan, al-Naml, al-Sajdah, Sad, Ha-Mim al-Sajdah, al-Najm, al-Inshiqaq and Iqra'."]),
171: ("On whom it is wajib, and on whom not", ["The prostration is wajib on whoever hears it, even if he did not mean to listen, except a woman in menstruation or nifas, and the imam and his followers hearing it from a follower. If they hear it from someone else, they prostrate after the prayer; if they prostrate during the prayer it does not count for them, though their prayer is not invalid, according to the apparent narration. It is wajib on hearing it in Persian if he understands it, according to the relied-upon view. Verdicts differ on whether it is wajib on hearing it from one asleep or insane. It is not wajib on hearing it from a bird or an echo."]),
172: ("How and when it is performed", ["In the prayer it is performed with a ruku' or prostration other than the ruku' and prostration of the prayer itself. The ruku' of the prayer suffices if he intends it, and its prostration suffices even if he does not intend it, as long as the recitation is not interrupted by more than two verses. If he hears it from an imam but does not follow him, or follows him in another rak'ah, he prostrates outside the prayer, according to the clearer view. If he follows him before the imam's prostration for it, he prostrates with him. If he follows him after its prostration in the same rak'ah, he counts as having caught it, so he does not prostrate it at all. A prostration due within the prayer is not made up outside it. If he recites outside the prayer, prostrates, then repeats it in the prayer, he prostrates again; if he did not prostrate the first time, one suffices, according to the apparent narration, as with one who repeats it in one sitting, but not in two sittings."]),
173: ("What changes the sitting", ["The sitting changes by leaving it, even if setting up a loom (moving back and forth), by moving from one branch to another, and by swimming in a river or a large pool, according to the soundest view."]),
174: ("What does not change the sitting", ["It does not change by moving between the corners of a house or masjid, even a large one; by the sailing of a ship; by a rak'ah or two, a drink, eating two mouthfuls or walking two steps; or by leaning, sitting, standing, mounting or dismounting in the place of his recitation; nor by his mount moving while he prays. The obligation is repeated for the listener if his sitting changes while the reciter's stays the same, but not the reverse, according to the soundest view."]),
175: ("Various rulings", ["It is disliked to recite a surah and leave out the verse of prostration, but not the reverse. It is recommended to add a verse or more to it, to recite it quietly in front of someone not ready for it, and to stand and then prostrate for it. The listener does not raise his head before the reciter. The reciter is not told to go forward, nor the listeners to form rows; they prostrate however they are."]),
176: ("Its conditions and how it is done", [
 "The conditions of the prayer are conditions for it to be valid, except the opening takbir.",
 "It is done by making one prostration between two takbirs, which are sunnah, without raising the hands, tashahhud or salam.",
]),
177: ("The prostration of thanks", [
 "The prostration of thanks is disliked according to Imam Abu Hanifah رحمه الله; there is no reward for it and leaving it is better.",
 "The two companions said it is an act of worship that is rewarded.",
 "It is done like the prostration of recitation.",
]),
178: ("An important benefit for every concern", [
 "Imam al-Nasafi said in al-Kafi:",
 "Whoever recites all the verses of prostration in one sitting and prostrates for each of them, Allah will suffice him in what concerns him.",
]),
179: ("The ruling of Jumu'ah", ["The Friday prayer is an individual obligation on everyone in whom seven conditions are found."]),
180: ("Conditions that make it obligatory", ["Being male, being free, residing in a town or in what counts as within its residence, according to the soundest view, health, safety from an oppressor, sound eyes and sound legs."]),
181: ("Conditions for it to be valid", [
 "Six things are conditions for it to be valid:",
 "a town or its outskirts; the ruler or his deputy; the time of Zuhr, so it is not valid before it and is cancelled by its ending; the khutbah before it, intended for it, within its time, with someone present to hear it of those with whom Jumu'ah is established, even one, according to the sound view; general permission (to attend); and the congregation, which is three men besides the imam, even if they are slaves, travellers or sick. The condition is that they remain with the imam until he prostrates. If they leave after his prostration, he completes it alone as Jumu'ah; if they leave before his prostration, it is cancelled. It is not valid with a woman or a boy together with two men. A slave or a sick man may lead it.",
 "A town is any place that has a mufti, an amir and a judge who carries out rulings and establishes the hudud, and whose buildings have reached those of Mina, according to the apparent narration. If the judge or amir is a mufti, that is enough. Jumu'ah is permitted at Mina in the season for the Caliph or the amir of the Hijaz.",
]),
182: ("The khutbah and its sunnahs", [
 "It is valid, with dislike, to limit the khutbah to something like one tasbih or tahmid.",
 "The sunnahs of the khutbah are eighteen: purity; covering the 'awrah; sitting on the minbar before beginning the khutbah; the adhan in front of him, like the iqamah; then standing, with the sword in his left hand leaning on it in every town taken by force, and without it in a town that made peace; facing the people; beginning with the praise of Allah and lauding Him as He deserves; the two testimonies; sending blessings upon our master the Prophet ﷺ; exhortation and reminder; reciting a verse of the Qur'an; two khutbahs; sitting between them; repeating the praise, laudation and blessings on our master the Prophet ﷺ at the start of the second khutbah; supplicating in it for the believers by asking forgiveness for them; the people hearing the khutbah; and keeping the two khutbahs short, the length of a surah of the long mufassal. Lengthening it, and leaving any of the sunnahs, are disliked.",
]),
183: ("Various rulings", [
 "It is wajib to hasten to Jumu'ah and to stop trading at the first adhan, according to the soundest view.",
 "When the imam comes out there is no prayer and no talk until he finishes his prayer. For one attending the khutbah, eating, drinking, fidgeting and turning around are disliked; he does not return a greeting or reply to one who sneezes. The khatib does not greet the people when he has settled on the minbar. It is disliked to leave the town after the call until one has prayed. If one not obliged to attend Jumu'ah performs it, it counts for the obligation of that time. If one with no excuse prays Zuhr before it, that is haram; then if he sets out for it while the imam is in it, his Zuhr is cancelled, even if he does not catch it. It is disliked for the excused and the imprisoned to pray Zuhr in congregation in the town on that day. Whoever catches it in the tashahhud or the prostration of forgetfulness completes it as Jumu'ah.",
]),
184: ("The 'Id prayers: ruling and conditions", ["The prayer of the two 'Ids is wajib, according to the soundest view, on whoever must pray Jumu'ah, with its conditions apart from the khutbah. It is valid without it, though wrongly, as it is if the khutbah is given before the 'Id prayer."]),
185: ("What is recommended on 'Id al-Fitr", [
 "Thirteen things are recommended on (the day of) al-Fitr:",
 "to eat (before going out), and that it be dates in an odd number; to make ghusl; to use the siwak; to wear perfume; to wear one's best clothes; to pay sadaqat al-fitr if it is due; to show joy and cheerfulness; to give much charity according to one's means; to rise early; to hasten to the prayer ground; to pray Fajr in the masjid of one's neighbourhood, then go to the prayer ground on foot, saying the takbir quietly and stopping when one reaches the prayer ground, according to one narration, and when the prayer begins, according to another; and to return by a different way.",
 "It is disliked to pray voluntary prayers before the 'Id prayer at the prayer ground and at home, and after it at the prayer ground only, according to the choice of the majority.",
]),
186: ("The time of the 'Id prayer", ["The time when the 'Id prayer is valid is from when the sun has risen the height of a spear or two until it passes its zenith."]),
187: ("How the 'Id prayer is prayed", [
 "He intends the 'Id prayer, then says the opening takbir, then recites the opening praise, then says the three extra takbirs, raising his hands in each, then seeks refuge, then says bismillah quietly, then recites al-Fatihah, then a surah, preferably \"Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High\", then bows.",
 "When he stands for the second rak'ah he begins with the basmalah, then al-Fatihah, then the surah, preferably al-Ghashiyah, then says the three extra takbirs, raising his hands as in the first. This is better than putting the extra takbirs before the recitation in the second rak'ah, but if he does put them before the recitation, it is permitted.",
 "Then after the prayer the imam gives two khutbahs, in which he teaches the rulings of sadaqat al-fitr. Whoever misses the prayer with the imam does not make it up. It may be postponed for an excuse to the next day only.",
]),
188: ("The rulings of al-Adha", ["The rulings of al-Adha are like those of al-Fitr, but on al-Adha he delays eating until after the prayer, says the takbir aloud on the way, and teaches about the sacrifice and the takbir of tashriq in the khutbah. It may be postponed for an excuse up to three days. The ta'rif (gathering on the day of 'Arafah outside 'Arafah) is nothing."]),
189: ("The takbir of tashriq: its ruling, period and on whom it is due", [
 "The takbir of tashriq is wajib from after Fajr on the day of 'Arafah until 'Asr on the day of 'Id, once straight after every obligatory prayer performed in a recommended congregation, on a resident imam in a town and on whoever follows him, even a traveller, a slave or a woman, according to Imam Abu Hanifah رحمه الله.",
 "The two companions said it is wajib straight after every obligatory prayer on whoever prays it, even alone, a traveller or a villager, until 'Asr of the fifth day counting from the day of 'Arafah; this is what is acted upon and fatwa is given on it. There is no harm in saying the takbir after the 'Id prayers.",
]),
190: ("The words of the takbir", ["The takbir is to say: \"Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, la ilaha illa'llah, wa'llahu akbar, Allahu akbar, wa lillahi'l-hamd.\""]),
191: ("Chapter: The prayer of the eclipse, the lunar eclipse and times of fear", [
 "The prayer of the solar eclipse",
 "Two rak'ahs like a voluntary prayer are sunnah for the solar eclipse, led by the imam of Jumu'ah or one appointed by the ruler, without adhan, iqamah, reciting aloud or khutbah; rather, the call \"al-salatu jami'ah\" is made.",
 "It is sunnah to make them long, with long ruku' and prostrations. Then the imam supplicates sitting facing the qiblah if he wishes, or standing facing the people, which is better, and they say amin to his supplication until the sun is fully clear.",
]),
192: ("The lunar eclipse, fear and the like", ["If the imam does not come, they pray individually, as for the lunar eclipse, terrifying darkness in the day, a severe wind and fear."]),
193: ("Chapter: Praying for rain (istisqa')", ["It has a prayer, without congregation, and it has seeking forgiveness."]),
194: ("What is done for it", [
 "It is recommended to go out for it for three days on foot, in worn clothes that are washed or patched, humbling themselves, lowly and humble before Allah the Exalted, with heads bowed, giving charity each day before they go out.",
 "It is recommended to bring out the animals, the very old and the children.",
 "In Makkah and Jerusalem they gather in al-Masjid al-Haram and al-Masjid al-Aqsa; and this should also be done by the people of the city of our master the Prophet ﷺ.",
]),
195: ("The supplication and how it is made", [
 "The imam stands facing the qiblah with his hands raised, and the people sit facing the qiblah, saying amin to his supplication. He says:",
 "\"O Allah, give us rain: a rescuing rain, wholesome and pleasant, bringing growth, abundant, swift and not delayed, covering everything, pouring, widespread and lasting\", and the like, quietly or aloud. There is no turning of the cloak in it, and no dhimmi attends it.",
]),
196: ("The prayer of fear: its ruling and cause", ["It is permitted when an enemy or a predator is present, and in fear of drowning or fire."]),
197: ("Leading it", ["If the people all want to pray behind one imam, he divides them into two groups: one faces the enemy, and he prays with the other one rak'ah of a two-rak'ah prayer, or two of a four-rak'ah prayer or Maghrib. This group goes on foot to face the enemy, and the other comes, and he prays with them what remains and gives salam alone. They go to the enemy, then the first group comes and completes the prayer without recitation, gives salam and goes. Then the other group comes, if they wish, and prays what remains with recitation."]),
198: ("When the fear is severe", [
 "If the fear is severe, they pray riding, individually, with gestures, in whatever direction they can.",
 "It is not permitted without an enemy present. It is recommended to carry weapons in the prayer in time of fear.",
 "If they do not all want to pray behind one imam, it is better for each group to pray with its own imam, as in times of safety.",
]),
199: ("What is done for one who is dying", [
 "It is sunnah to lay the dying person on his right side, facing the qiblah; lying on his back is permitted, with his head raised a little. The shahadah is prompted by saying it near him, without insisting, and he is not told to say it. Prompting him (talqin) in the grave is legislated; it is also said that he is not prompted, and that one is neither told to do it nor forbidden from it.",
 "It is recommended for the relatives and neighbours of the dying person to visit him and recite Surah Ya-Sin near him; some later scholars approved of Surat al-Ra'd. They differed about having women in menstruation or nifas leave his presence.",
]),
}
